  "RECONSTRUCTED by the site row: One gable over the body, ridge east-west with its gable to Indiana Avenue, as the Otis 1887 plate shows it (sheet 35 draws dashed hips, which the photograph does not bear out). Through other work's camera the gable springs at the north kneeler, 2.0 m in from the body's north wall over a narrow bay with its own flat roof and stack, and rises 16.0 m over its 10.6 m half-width to its coping's apex at about 30.9 m, so it stands at 56.5 degrees. The tower is the trace's square at the south-west corner, 6.0 m across its faces, its brick to 39.5 m where the photograph's gablets spring, with the slated octagonal spire out of them. The sheet's printed 90 ft (27.4 m) cannot be the spire's height over grade: the photograph puts the tip at five times the eave's height, the sheet's own 48 ft. Other work dresses the front and the tower to the plate (the gothic church feature; every place, width and height is read off the photograph at 18.7 px/m on the front and 18.2 on the tower, and the match record names each): the coping on its corbel table with kneelers and the cross; the gable's rose, the centre window under a rose head, the two side lancets, the north window and the two small lancets at the north foot, the sill and base courses; the gabled stone porch and its pointed doorway; the tower's pointed door and panel, its two-light window with a rose head, its louvred belfry, its courses, its angle buttresses with their gabled offsets and corner pinnacles, and four gablets; and the spire's taper (5.5 m across at its foot, 3.0 m at the band, 1.1 m at its flat top at 71.8 m), its band of lucarnes at 57.4-59.5 m and the finial's knob, fleur and rod to 78.1 m. What the frontal plate cannot give is reconstructed: the porch's 2.0 m depth, the buttresses' projections, the openings and gablets on the tower's east and north faces (the belfry on all four, the second stage's window on the west and south only, as seen), and the north-west buttress's depth, set 1.5 m proud of the front because its shadow falls on the front wall. The book's 1912 plate (\"First Presbyterian Church. Indiana Avenue and Twenty-First Street (1912).\"…), taken from Indiana Avenue's roadway about 66 m south of 21st Street looking north-north-east with the camera tilted up, shows the tower's south and west faces, the west gable edge-on with its cross, and the nave's south roof slope to the east gable; it does not show the 21st Street flank, which the low buildings across 21st Street and a streetcar screen below the eaves. Read through its camera it adds: two gabled ventilators on the south slope, about 3.6 m wide, their faces standing on the slope at 23.15 m and rising to about 26.3 m, centred 21.5 m and 32.2 m behind the front; a short stack on the east gable's apex to about 32.4 m; and the cross over the west gable to 34.65 m, where both plates put its top (the 1887 plate's crossbar is the 33.0 m first read as its top). The ventilators' depth into the roof and their faces' material are reconstructed, and the north slope, which neither plate sees, is given none. The 21st Street flank and the rear keep the draft's sash: no picture read so far shows them. The seed 72272670 only tints the fabric inside +-6 %. Liberty “The background behind Prairie Avenue continued: Indiana Avenue's east frontage, 20th to 22nd Street, stood up as drafts where Prairie's sidewalks see it, with the First Presbyterian Church on the 21st Street corner”.": {
